course: english
lesson: 37
title: Paragraphs and cohesion: holding a text together
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A paragraph is a group of sentences about one thing, and starting a new one tells the reader "something has changed", like a new scene in a film.
explain: Many teachers use TiPToP to know when to start a new paragraph: a change of Time, Place, Topic or Person; a Year 3 story written as one giant block is like a lunchbox with everything mashed together.
explain: Cohesion is the glue that makes the sentences and paragraphs hang together: pronouns (Max ... he), linking words (because, however), time and place openers (Later that day, Meanwhile, Outside) and repeated key words all hold the reader's hand.
explain: "Then... then... then..." is weak glue: it only says "next" and soon sounds like a shopping list; "After lunch", "Suddenly" or "By the time we got home" also tell the reader when and how.
explain: In information texts, headings such as "Habitat", "Diet" and "Life cycle" and signposts such as "Firstly", "Secondly" and "Finally" act like signs in a big shop that tell you which aisle you are in.
fact: A paragraph is a group of sentences about one main idea.
fact: TiPToP is a way to remember when to start a new paragraph: a change of Time, Place, Topic or Person.
fact: A new paragraph is shown by an indent (a small gap at the start of the line) or by a blank line; schools and books use one or the other.
fact: The first sentence of a paragraph often tells the reader what it is about; it is called a topic sentence.
fact: Cohesion means the parts of a text are linked so that it reads as one whole.
fact: Pronouns such as he, she, it and they avoid repeating names, but they must be clear: "Tom met Sam and he laughed" does not say who laughed.
fact: Adverbials such as "Later that day", "Meanwhile" and "Nearby" link paragraphs by time and place.
fact: Linking words such as "however", "because" and "as a result" show how ideas connect.
fact: Repeating a key word, or a synonym for it, helps link paragraphs about the same topic.
fact: In non-fiction, headings, subheadings and signposts such as "Firstly, secondly, finally" organise the text.
fact: A long paragraph is not wrong, but a paragraph that mixes several ideas is hard to follow.
fact: In Leeds and other parts of Northern England "well" means very, as in "well long"; in Gen Alpha slang "slay" means do brilliantly.
exercise: A Year 3 story is one giant block. What would help the reader most? | Paragraphs where something changes | Writing the whole thing again in capital letters | Adding more "and"s to join every sentence up | Making the handwriting much smaller to fit
exercise: What does TiPToP stand for? | Time, Place, Topic, Person | Title, Picture, Text, Page | Tidy, Polite, Tall, Proud | Tense, Pronoun, Tone, Punctuation
exercise: Ava writes "Then we ate. Then we played. Then we went home." Which is the best improvement? | After lunch we played, and by four we were home. | Then we ate and then we played and then we went home. | Then, then, then, we went home. | We ate. We played. We went home.
exercise: Year 5: "Use pronouns to avoid repeating names." Which sentence does it well? | Max opened the box and he gasped. | Max opened the box and Max gasped at Max's box. | He opened the box and he gasped, Max. | Max opened the box and it gasped.
exercise: Liam from Leeds says "My paragraphs are well long." His teacher says "Long isn't bad, but split ideas." What does "well long" mean? | Very long | Long and good | Not long at all | Long ago
exercise: In a Year 6 report, what do "Firstly, secondly, finally" do? | Signpost the order of the points | Show where the speech marks go | Replace the need for paragraphs | Make the report sound like a story
exercise: Tom, Gen Alpha, says "My paragraphs slay." What is he saying? | His paragraphs are brilliant | His paragraphs are far too short | His paragraphs are frightening | His paragraphs are about dragons
exercise: Mum says "Indent the start." The child asks why. What is the best answer? | It shows the reader a new paragraph begins | It makes the page look emptier and faster to read | Teachers like seeing empty space | It means the paragraph is not important
exercise: A Year 5 report on frogs has the headings "Habitat", "Diet" and "Life cycle". Why? | Each heading tells the reader what that part is about | Headings make the whole report count as a made-up story | Headings are where the best facts are always hidden | Headings replace all the sentences
exercise: The teacher says "Repeat key words to link paragraphs." Which ending of paragraph one links best to paragraph two about the frog's food? | Damp places are also full of food. | Frogs need damp places, and that is the end of my report. | Frogs need damp places, but honestly who cares about that. | Anyway, frogs are green and some of them can jump far.
exercise: Which of these is a time adverbial that links paragraphs? | Later that afternoon | Under the kitchen table | Very quickly and quietly | The big red double-decker bus
exercise: In a story, the hero walks from the beach into a cave. What should happen in the writing? | A new paragraph, as the place changes | Nothing, because places don't matter in stories | A brand new chapter for every single step | A long list of everything inside the cave
exercise: What is a topic sentence? | The sentence that says what a paragraph is about | The very last sentence in a whole book or chapter | A sentence that asks the reader a hard question | Any sentence that comes straight after a heading
exercise: "Tom met Sam and he laughed." What is the problem? | We can't tell who laughed | "Met" is in the wrong tense | It needs a semicolon after "Tom" | "Laughed" needs a capital letter
exercise: Which word shows a contrast between two paragraphs? | However | Firstly | Also | Next
exercise: Which change would need a new paragraph by TiPToP? | A new character starts speaking | A new adjective is used in the sentence | A comma appears in the middle | The sentence gets much longer than before
test: Your mate goes "paragraphs are just there so the page looks less scary, bruv." What is the truth? | They group ideas so the reader can follow | They are only there to look nice on the page | They are only needed in poems and songs | They show the writer is tired
test: ★★★ A story goes: (1) At home, Mia packs. (2) On the train, she reads. (3) Still on the train, a man sits down. (4) The next morning, in Paris, she wakes. How many paragraphs by TiPToP, if a new person arriving counts? | Four | Three | Two | One
test: In a cartoon, a boy writes his whole holiday diary as one line on a long roll of paper. What would a teacher tell him? | Break it into paragraphs by day and place | Use a longer roll so it fits | Use smaller letters to save paper | Write it backwards for fun
test: What would happen if a writer used "he" all the way through a story about three boys? | Readers would get confused about who does what | The story would be much clearer and a lot shorter to read | It would become a non-fiction report | Nothing, readers can always guess
test: A Scottish nan says "Yer story's braw, hen, but it's aw wan lump." ("Braw" is lovely, "hen" a friendly word, "aw wan lump" is all one lump.) What does she want? | Paragraphs | More pictures | A shorter title | Bigger handwriting
test: ★★★ Which sentence starts a paragraph that clearly follows one about breakfast? | After breakfast, we set off for the lake. | We set off for the lake. | Then then we set off. | The lake is a big lake.
test: Your brother, bit sharp: "Stop starting every sentence with 'then', it's doing my head in." What could you use instead? | Time openers like "After that" or "By noon" | More "then"s, but written in big capital letters | Nothing, "then" is the only option | Question marks after each "then"
test: A boy in Georgia writes "Y'all, I got three reasons." ("Y'all" means you all.) In a formal report, which opening works better? | There are three main reasons. | Y'all, here's my reasons, innit. | Reasons, reasons, reasons. | Three things or whatever.
test: ★★★ In a report on owls, paragraph 2 is about food and paragraph 3 begins "Their sharp claws are not only for hunting." Why is that a clever link? | It joins the food idea to the new topic of body | It repeats the title of the whole report | It starts a new story about owls | It shows the report has ended
test: A cousin from Sydney says "My report's got heaps of headings, mate." ("Heaps" means lots.) When are headings most useful? | In information texts, to sort facts by topic | In every line of a story, to make it look neat | Only in poems, above each verse | Only at the end, as a summary of everything
test: Which ordering signposts belong together in a non-fiction text? | Firstly, secondly, finally | Once, upon, time | However, cats, purr | Suddenly, crash, bang
exam: Identify the principal function of a paragraph. | To group sentences concerning a single main idea | To indicate that a sentence is a question | To mark the omission of letters | To separate the items of a list
exam: Which of the following most accurately characterises the mnemonic "TiPToP"? | A reminder to commence a new paragraph upon a change of time, place, topic or person | A rule for the placement of apostrophes | A method for the spelling of plurals | A list of the four types of sentence
exam: Select the response consistent with established conventions concerning cohesion. | Pronouns, adverbials and repeated key words link a text together | Cohesion is achieved solely by the use of full stops | Cohesion requires every sentence to begin with "then" | Cohesion is relevant only to poetry
exam: Ascertain the defect in the sentence "Tom met Sam and he laughed". | The referent of "he" is ambiguous | The verb "met" is in the future tense | The sentence lacks a verb | The sentence contains a comma splice
